## Membrane Casting

The polymer solution was blade-cast onto the substrate at a controlled pulling speed of **1 mm/s**. Following casting, the membrane coupon was exposed to a **laminar dry nitrogen purge** for **355 seconds** prior to bath immersion. This nitrogen blow step was employed to minimize atmospheric humidity exposure and to control the evaporation dynamics at the film surface during the pre-immersion period.

## Phase Separation & Precipitation

After the pre-immersion waiting period, the cast film was immersed into the non-solvent (water) bath, maintained at a reduced temperature of **5°C**. The membrane was allowed to undergo phase separation and precipitation in the bath for **1800 seconds (30 minutes)**, enabling complete solidification of the polymer matrix.

## Notes

The combination of a relatively low casting speed, extended nitrogen purge time, and cold bath temperature suggests this protocol is designed to investigate the effects of controlled solvent evaporation and suppressed phase separation kinetics on the resulting membrane morphology.
